# Spacetime Support And Microlocal Projections

The previous chapter made cones and incidence lawful. This chapter teaches what
can be supported by those cones before the operator/action/curvature page begins.

The claim-site carried here is:

* claim-site: spacetime support, singular support, causal microsupport,
  cotangent codirections, charged microsupport, and causal polarization;
* phase: relational spacetime;
* generating sites: continuous closure flow, NSC firing, causal closure, closed
  future cone, direct causal edge, edge cochain, FOUR-valued charge emission,
  proper coloring, and bipartite obstruction;
* downstream uses: causal operators, curvature/action readings, spacetime
  cohomology, plasma and wavefront diagnostics, quantum support gluing,
  finite infohole shrouds, and frontier symplectic-flow claims;
* Lean carriers: `Infomaterial.Relational.Spacetime.Support.Microlocal`,
  `Infomaterial.Relational.Spacetime.Support.Causal`,
  `Infomaterial.Relational.Spacetime.Support.Cotangent`,
  `Infomaterial.Relational.Spacetime.Support.Charged`, and
  `Infomaterial.Relational.Spacetime.Support.Polarization`;
* Blueprint/proof-tree nodes: `thm:microlocal-support`,
  `thm:causal-microsupport`, `thm:cotangent-microsupport`,
  `thm:charged-microsupport`, and `thm:causal-polarization`;
* provenance: the causal-spacetime frontier asks for a light-cone,
  wavefront, charge, and polarization layer before continuum physics is read;
* familiar comparison: microlocal singular support, propagation of singularities,
  AQFT locality, cotangent wavefront set, and symplectic polarization;
* reader closure: read every classical term through the finite generated
  carrier named beside it, not through a hidden manifold;
* frontier status: there is no smooth cotangent bundle, PDE propagation
  theorem, AQFT net, symplectic two-form, or continuum wavefront set here.
  Promotion requires those carriers and comparison theorems.

The governing sentence is:

> Support is causal closure with enough extra structure to remember where,
> in which codirection, with which charge, and under which polarization.

## Closure-Flow Singular Support

A closure-flow singularity is an NSC firing:

{docstring Infomaterial.Relational.Spacetime.Support.Microlocal.SingularAt}

and smoothness means that no such firing occurs:

{docstring Infomaterial.Relational.Spacetime.Support.Microlocal.SmoothFlow}

Continuous closure flow has empty singular support:

{docstring Infomaterial.Relational.Spacetime.Support.Microlocal.continuous_empty_singular_support}

Checked:

```lean
example (w : Valuation.ModalFrame.OrdFlow)
    (hw : Valuation.ModalFrame.Continuous w) :
    SmoothFlow w :=
  continuous_empty_singular_support w hw
```

This is the first support claim. It does not say that a smooth manifold
singularity has vanished. It says that the closure flow has no NSC firing.

The bundled support projection also ties causal propagation to closure and reads
`H1` vanishing as the flat side of the curvature regime:

{docstring Infomaterial.Relational.Spacetime.Support.Microlocal.microlocal_support_propagation}

Checked:

```lean
example {E : Type} (C : CausalSet E) (r : ClosureRegime) :
    (∀ S : Section E,
      causalClosure C
        (causalClosure C S) = causalClosure C S)
    ∧ (cohomologyVanishes r = true ↔
      Flow.CurvatureRegime.IsFlat r)
    ∧ (cohomologyVanishes r = false ↔
      Flow.CurvatureRegime.IsCurved r) :=
  microlocal_support_propagation C r
```

The lawful content is small and strong: propagation is a closure operator, and
the curvature regime carries the settled `H1` classification. The word
"microlocal" is a projection of that internal support claim.

## Causal Microsupport

The causal microsupport of a section is its causal closure:

{docstring Infomaterial.Relational.Spacetime.Support.Causal.Microsupport}

It is the union of closed future cones:

{docstring Infomaterial.Relational.Spacetime.Support.Causal.microsupport_iff_union_cones}

and it is upward closed in the causal order:

{docstring Infomaterial.Relational.Spacetime.Support.Causal.microsupport_upward_closed}

Re-propagating adds nothing:

{docstring Infomaterial.Relational.Spacetime.Support.Causal.microsupport_idem}

Checked:

```lean
example {E : Type} (C : CausalSet E) (S : Section E) :
    Microsupport C (Microsupport C S) = Microsupport C S :=
  microsupport_idem C S
```

A point source fills exactly its closed future cone:

{docstring Infomaterial.Relational.Spacetime.Support.Causal.microsupport_point}

Checked:

```lean
example {E : Type} (C : CausalSet E) (a : E) :
    Microsupport C (fun x => x = a) = ClosedFuture C a :=
  microsupport_point C a
```

Spacelike locality is then a theorem about non-reachability, not an imported
commutation axiom:

{docstring Infomaterial.Relational.Spacetime.Support.Causal.causal_microsupport_unifies}

The page may now say "propagation" and "locality," but only because the causal
closure site has generated them.

## Cotangent Codirections

The cotangent layer starts with directed causal edges:

{docstring Infomaterial.Relational.Spacetime.Support.Cotangent.Codir}

Their source and target are the finite bundle projection and edge tip:

{docstring Infomaterial.Relational.Spacetime.Support.Cotangent.codSource}

{docstring Infomaterial.Relational.Spacetime.Support.Cotangent.codTarget}

A codirection is characteristic by construction:

{docstring Infomaterial.Relational.Spacetime.Support.Cotangent.codir_characteristic}

and it reaches its target in one causal step:

{docstring Infomaterial.Relational.Spacetime.Support.Cotangent.codir_reaches}

This is not a smooth cotangent bundle. It is the direct-edge codirection carrier
that the causal order can actually generate.

## Wavefront And Singular Projection

An edge cochain is the discrete covector field:

{docstring Infomaterial.Relational.Spacetime.Support.Cotangent.EdgeCochain}

The wavefront is its codirection support:

{docstring Infomaterial.Relational.Spacetime.Support.Cotangent.Wavefront}

The base singular support is the projection of that wavefront:

{docstring Infomaterial.Relational.Spacetime.Support.Cotangent.SingSupport}

Checked:

```lean
example {E : Type} (C : CausalSet E)
    (phi : EdgeCochain C) (e : E) :
    SingSupport C phi e ↔
      ∃ xi : Codir C,
        codSource C xi = e ∧ phi xi ≠ 0 :=
  singSupport_iff_projection C phi e
```

Empty wavefront gives a trivial cohomology class:

{docstring Infomaterial.Relational.Spacetime.Support.Cotangent.wavefront_empty_coboundary}

Checked:

```lean
example {E : Type} (C : CausalSet E)
    (phi : EdgeCochain C)
    (h : ∀ xi, ¬ Wavefront C phi xi) :
    IsCoboundary C phi :=
  wavefront_empty_coboundary C phi h
```

The `clinv` bridge is explicitly finite:

{docstring Infomaterial.Relational.Spacetime.Support.Cotangent.clinv_nonzero_singular}

The bundled theorem is:

{docstring Infomaterial.Relational.Spacetime.Support.Cotangent.cotangent_microsupport}

The frontier line is important. A codirection support can project to a base
singularity. That is not yet a Hamiltonian bicharacteristic flow on a symplectic
cotangent bundle.

## Charged Microsupport

Causal support can now be valued in the FOUR:

{docstring Infomaterial.Relational.Spacetime.Support.Charged.CSection}

A source emits a charge into its causal future and bottom outside it:

{docstring Infomaterial.Relational.Spacetime.Support.Charged.emit}

Two emissions merge by knowledge join:

{docstring Infomaterial.Relational.Spacetime.Support.Charged.emit2}

Outside the cone, a source tells nothing:

{docstring Infomaterial.Relational.Spacetime.Support.Charged.emit_outside_cone}

Checked:

```lean
example {E : Type} (le : E -> E -> Bool)
    (a : E) (q : Charge) (z : E)
    (h : le a z = false) :
    emit le a q z = Charge.kBot :=
  emit_outside_cone le a q z h
```

Contradictory emissions that meet in a common future hold conflict:

{docstring Infomaterial.Relational.Spacetime.Support.Charged.collision_conflict}

Checked:

```lean
example {E : Type} (le : E -> E -> Bool)
    (a1 a2 z : E)
    (h1 : le a1 z = true) (h2 : le a2 z = true) :
    emit2 le a1 Charge.tTop a2 Charge.tBot z
      = Charge.kTop :=
  collision_conflict le a1 a2 z h1 h2
```

The bundled charged theorem is:

{docstring Infomaterial.Relational.Spacetime.Support.Charged.charged_microsupport}

This is the lawful place where the cone and the FOUR meet. It does not create
new truth values; it transports the earlier charge carrier through causal
support.

## Causal Polarization

The polarization form is the color difference across a direct causal edge:

{docstring Infomaterial.Relational.Spacetime.Support.Polarization.polForm}

Nondegeneracy means that no direct causal edge is collapsed:

{docstring Infomaterial.Relational.Spacetime.Support.Polarization.Nondegenerate}

This is exactly proper coloring:

{docstring Infomaterial.Relational.Spacetime.Support.Polarization.nondegenerate_iff_proper}

Checked:

```lean
example {E : Type} (C : CausalSet E) (c : E -> Int) :
    Nondegenerate C c ↔ ProperColoring C.prec0 c :=
  nondegenerate_iff_proper C c
```

A factor map preserves the polarization:

{docstring Infomaterial.Relational.Spacetime.Support.Polarization.mld_preserves_polarization}

The Boolean obstruction is bipartiteness:

{docstring Infomaterial.Relational.Spacetime.Support.Polarization.exists_bool_polarization_iff_bipartite}

Checked:

```lean
example {E : Type} (C : CausalSet E) :
    (∃ c : E -> Bool, ProperColoring C.prec0 c)
      ↔ Bipartite C.prec0 :=
  exists_bool_polarization_iff_bipartite C
```

The bundled theorem is:

{docstring Infomaterial.Relational.Spacetime.Support.Polarization.causal_polarization}

The familiar symplectic reading is a projection. The theorem settles the conjugate
split as a coloring carrier and its obstruction as a graph-theoretic class.

## Repair Path

If this page overreaches, demote the sentence until a carrier exists. The
promotion path is:

1. support claims must name the closure, section, codirection, charge, or color;
2. microlocal claims must be checked against `SingularAt`, `Microsupport`,
   `Wavefront`, or `SingSupport`;
3. physical locality claims must pass through causal non-reachability or
   charge-valued emission;
4. symplectic claims must pass through polarization and then wait for a genuine
   two-form and flow carrier;
5. infohole or shroud claims may use this support layer only after the finite
   no-readout region is generated.

The next chapter may turn incidence into operators and curvature, but only
because this page has first separated support from operator action.
